{"name":"Natural Radiance","hex":"#e7dcc1","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"natural","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Existing in nature.","Normally associated with a particular person or thing; inherently related to the nature of a thing or creature."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native inhabitant of a place, country, etc.","A note that is not or is no longer to be modified by an accidental."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/natural"},{"term":"radiance","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The quality or state of being radiant; shining, bright or splendid.","The flux of radiation emitted per unit solid angle in a given direction by a unit area of a source."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/radiance"}],"entries":[{"title":"Radiance","blocks":[{"text":"In radiometry, radiance is the radiant flux emitted, reflected, transmitted or received by a given surface, per unit solid angle per unit projected area. Radiance is used to characterize diffuse emission and reflection of electromagnetic radiation, and to quantify emission of neutrinos and other particles. The SI unit of radiance is the watt per steradian per square metre. It is a directional quantity: the radiance of a surface depends on the direction from which it is being observed."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radiance","wikidata":"Q1411145"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"natural":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-natural.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/3/35/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-natural.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-natural.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.o